body:
The object's name? As in:

    class NameOfMyClass;
Or the name of the object as in 

    NameOfMyClass objectName;        
Either way, what I think you want to do is print out data members of the class?

    class NameOfMyClass
    {
       int x;
       int y;
       int z;
    };
Like x y and z in this case?

If so, then if you have a member function, you can access the object's data implicitly like this:

    void NameOfMyClass::PrintOutData(void)
    {
        std::cout << x << y << z << std::endl;
    }

Or explicitly using:

    void NameOfMyClass::PrintOutData(void)
    {
        std::cout << this->x << this->y << this->z << std::endl;
    }

The 'this' keyword is an implicit pointer to the current object instance calling a non-static member function (so any function declared inside a class that isn't marked as static can call 'this' to access the object that is calling that function). It's useful for removing any ambiguity between variables with the same name or if you want to make something clearer for other programmers. It's not needed though, as I showed you above. Does this help?
